he science of\n    gravimetry. The Galileo is defined as $1 \\textit{centimeter per square second}$ ($1 cm/s^2$). Unfortunately, the Galileo is\n    often denoted with the symbol Gal, not to be confused with the Gallon that also uses the same symbol.\n  ","@type":"http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/LatexString"}],"http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/applicableSystem":[{"@id":"http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS"},{"@id":"http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS-GAUSS"}],"http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/conversionMultiplier":[{"@value":"0.01","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal"}],"http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/conversionMultiplierSN":[{"@value":"1.0E-2","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double"}],"http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/dbpediaMatch":[{"@value":"http://dbpedia.org/resource/Gal","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI"}],"http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/derivedUnitOfSystem":[{"@id":"http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS"},{"@id":"http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS-GAUSS"}],"http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/hasDimensionVector":[{"@id":"http://qudt.org/vocab/dimensionvector/A0E0L1I0M0H0T-2D0"}],"http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/hasFactorUnit":[{"@id":"_:n3-1396"},{"@id":"_:n3-1397"}],"http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/hasQuantityKind":[{"@id":"http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/Acceleration"},{"@id":"http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/LinearAcceleration"}],"http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/iec61360Code":[{"@value":"0112/2///62720#UAB042","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"}],"http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/informativeReference":[{"@value":"https://cdd.iec.ch/cdd/iec62720/iec62720.nsf/Units/0112-2---62720%23UAB042","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI"},{"@value":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gal?oldid=482010741","@type":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI"}],"http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/omUnit":[{"@id":"http://www.ontology-of-units-of-measure.org/resource/om-2/gal"}],"http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/plainTextDescription":[{"@value":"CGS unit of acceleration called gal with the defini